Ladybird now decides if focus that a script moves matches :focus-visible
from how the user last moved focus, and a keydown without Ctrl, Alt, or
Super held cancels a click’s effect. Blink does the same: Its Document::
SetFocusedElement() records no LastFocusType() for focus by script, and
SelectorChecker::MatchesFocusVisiblePseudoClass() doesn’t match after a
mouse focus unless HadKeyboardEvent(), which KeyboardEventManager::
KeyEvent() sets on a keydown without Ctrl, Alt, or Meta held. WebKit’s
Document::setFocusedElement() leaves m_latestFocusTrigger alone for
focus from bindings, Element::focus() makes that focus invisible if
wasLastFocusByClick(), and EventHandler::internalKeyEvent() resets the
trigger on a keyboard interaction. And Gecko’s ShouldMatchFocusVisible()
matches focus with an unknown cause only if the last known focus method
was by key or there was none (UnknownFocusMethodShouldShowOutline()).

Keyboard use also updates the focused element’s :focus-visible at once,
as Blink’s Node::UpdateHadKeyboardEvent() does by calling the element’s
FocusVisibleStateChanged() and WebKit’s EventHandler::internalKeyEvent()
does by calling the element’s setHasFocusVisible(). And focus()’s
focusVisible option overrides every heuristic, as it does in all three
engines: Blink’s MatchesFocusVisiblePseudoClass() checks focus options
first, WebKit’s Element::focus() turns the option into a Visible or a
ForceInvisible focus visibility, and Gecko’s ShouldMatchFocusVisible()
checks FLAG_SHOWRING and FLAG_NOSHOWRING before any other heuristic.
